course: everyday
lesson: 39
title: Time, routines and sleep
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A routine is the same set of steps done in the same order every day, like getting up, washing, dressing, eating breakfast and packing your bag. Because you do not have to decide each step, routines save time and stop you forgetting things.
explain: Sleep is when your body grows and repairs itself and your brain sorts and stores what you learned. Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep each night.
explain: Your body has an inner clock that likes regular times. Going to bed and getting up at about the same time every day, even at weekends, makes it easier to fall asleep and to wake up.
explain: For example, a boy in Atlanta must leave for school at 7:45. Breakfast takes 20 minutes and washing and dressing take 25 minutes, so he must get up by 7:00. Planning backward from the time you must leave is the trick.
explain: Bright screens and exciting games just before bed keep the brain alert, so it is wise to put them away about an hour before sleep. Big homework jobs are easier broken into small parts done on different days.
explain: A boy in Texas stayed up past midnight every school night; he got grumpy, forgot things, and fell off his bike because he was too tired to concentrate.
explain: A girl in Dublin always left in a rush and ran across the road to catch the bus; one morning she nearly stepped in front of a van.
fact: A routine is a set of steps done in the same order every day.
fact: Routines save time and help you remember things.
fact: Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep each night.
fact: During sleep the body grows and repairs itself and the brain stores what you have learned.
fact: Going to bed and getting up at regular times helps the body's inner clock.
fact: Bright screens and exciting games before bed make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: You should brush your teeth for about two minutes, twice a day.
fact: To be on time, you plan backward from the time you have to leave.
fact: A big task is easier when it is broken into smaller parts done over several days.
fact: There are 60 minutes in an hour and 24 hours in a day.
fact: Packing your bag the night before saves time in the morning.
fact: Adults usually need about 7 to 9 hours of sleep a night, so children need more sleep than adults.
fact: Tired, rushed people make more mistakes and have more accidents.
exercise: How many hours of sleep do children aged 6 to 12 need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | Only about 4 to 5 hours | Just about 6 to 7 hours | About 15 to 16 hours
exercise: Why does a morning routine help you? | You do not have to stop and decide each step | It makes the clock move more slowly | It means you can skip breakfast | It lets you stay in bed much longer every single morning
exercise: Why should you put screens away about an hour before bed? | Bright light and excitement keep the brain alert | Screens use up the electricity you need at night | Screens make your eyes too heavy to open | Screens make the bedroom too warm for sleep
exercise: What does the body do while you sleep? | Grows, repairs itself and stores what you learned | Switches off completely until morning | Forgets everything from the day | Uses up all the food from dinner at once, so you wake hungry
exercise: Why is it good to go to bed at the same time every night? | It helps your body's inner clock | It makes the night shorter | It lets you need less sleep each night | It stops you from dreaming
exercise: You must leave at 8:15 and need 30 minutes to get ready. When must you start? | 7:45 | 7:15 | 8:00 | 8:45
exercise: A project is due in a week. What is the smartest plan? | Do a small part of it each day | Do all of it in one go the night before | Wait until someone reminds you about it | Do it only if there is spare time
exercise: How long should you brush your teeth each time? | About two minutes | Just ten seconds or so | About ten whole minutes | Only until they feel wet
exercise: Why does packing your school bag the night before help? | The morning is less rushed and you forget less | Books are lighter at night | It makes the bus come sooner | Teachers check bags in the evening before school starts
exercise: A girl in Colorado sleeps from 9:00 p.m. to 7:00 a.m. How long does she sleep? | 10 hours | 9 and a half hours | 12 whole hours | Only 2 hours
exercise: Why does sleeping late every weekend make Monday mornings hard? | It upsets the body's inner clock | It uses up all your sleep for the week | Monday mornings are always darker | The alarm is quieter on Mondays
exercise: How many minutes are in two hours? | 120 | 100 | 60 | 200
exercise: Why can it be hard to learn well after a very short night's sleep? | The brain has not had time to rest and store learning | Sleep makes the brain forget everything | Tired brains learn twice as fast | Lessons go more slowly when you are tired
test: A boy in Auckland plays an exciting video game until he goes to bed and then lies awake. What is the best explanation? | The game kept his brain too alert to sleep | Games make you sleepy, so he was too sleepy | His room was too dark to sleep in | He had eaten too much protein at lunch
test: Which statement is true? | Children usually need more sleep than adults | Children need less sleep because they are small | Everyone needs exactly 8 hours, at any age | Sleep is a waste of time for the brain
test: Lily's bus comes at 8:10. She needs 15 minutes for breakfast and 25 minutes to wash and dress. What is the latest she can get up? | 7:30 | 7:45 | 7:55 | 7:20
test: What would most likely happen if you started a big science project the night before it was due? | You would rush and probably do a poor job | You would always do your best work | The teacher would give you an extra day or two | The project would take less time
test: A girl in Dublin goes to bed at 11 p.m. and gets up at 7 a.m. on school days. What is the problem? | She sleeps 8 hours, less than a child her age needs | She sleeps far too much for her age | She sleeps exactly as much as a child needs | There is no problem at all with this
test: Why do people use alarm clocks? | To wake at the same planned time every day | To make the night pass more quickly while you sleep | To help them fall asleep | To tell them when to eat lunch
test: Jake keeps forgetting his PE kit. What would help most? | A routine of packing his bag each night | Buying a bigger bag for school | Going to bed much later | Leaving his kit at home on purpose so it can't get lost
test: Why is it smart to plan backward from the time you have to leave? | You can work out when each step must start | It makes the clock run slower | It means you can skip some steps | It makes everyone else late instead of you, so it's fair
test: Which is the best bedtime routine? | Bath, teeth, a book and lights out at the same time | Games on a phone until you fall asleep | A big sugary snack and a scary film | A different bedtime every single night
test: need to leave at 8, takes 30 min to get ready, start when | 7:30 | 8:30 | 7:00 | 8:00
test: project due friday, it's massive | Break it into small parts over several days | Do it all on thursday night | Leave it all till friday morning before school | Do one huge bit and stop
test: how long do i brush my teeth | About two minutes | Just a quick ten seconds | A good ten minutes or more | Until the paste runs out
test: pack ur bag tonight, why | It saves time in the morning | Bags are easier to pack at night | Teachers check your bag at night | It makes the bag lighter to carry
test: been up til 1am all week, why can't i focus | Your brain needs more sleep to work well | Focus has nothing to do with sleep | You need more fizzy drinks | You should stay up later to get used to it
test: late for the bus, gonna sprint across the road | Stop and cross safely, it is better to miss the bus | Run, drivers will stop | Run without looking to save time | Run and look after you are across
test: Ivy says kids only need 6 hrs sleep like her dad. true? | No, children need about 9 to 12 hours | Yes, everyone needs exactly the same amount | Yes, sleep is a waste of time | Yes, unless it is the weekend
test: heard a kid stayed up all night gaming before his test and fell asleep in it and got zero | Real lesson: sleep helps the brain store learning; the zero part may be made up | Real lesson: tests should be held in the evening | It proves games make you clever | It is gossip, so sleep does not matter
test: my mate says brushing ur teeth for 10 seconds is enough if u do it hard | Not enough; brush for about two minutes, twice a day | He is right, hard brushing beats long brushing | He is right, ten seconds is the rule | He is right, once a week is fine
test: Ben's always late and people say his family are lazy, pass it on | Do not pass it on; you do not know why, and it is unkind | Pass it on, so he gets up earlier | Pass it on, since being late proves it | Pass it on, and add his mum is late too
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the functions of sleep? | During sleep the body grows and repairs itself and the brain stores what has been learned | During sleep the body and brain cease all activity | Sleep serves only to rest the eyes | Sleep is required only by small children
exam: It being given that a child must depart at eight o'clock, identify the method by which punctuality is best assured. | Planning backward from the time of departure | Commencing preparations at the time of departure | Waiting until reminded by an adult | Preparing items in a random order
exam: Identify the number of minutes contained in three hours. | One hundred and eighty minutes | Thirty minutes | Sixty minutes | Three hundred minutes
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning a large task. | It is rendered easier by division into smaller parts undertaken over several days | It is best completed in its entirety on the final night | It should be avoided until it is no longer required | It should be done without any planning at all
